VERDICT
Correct: your code successfully finds the minimum and maximum values and computes their greatest common divisor, but the GCD calculation uses a slow manual loop instead of standard math algorithms.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: You found both the minimum and maximum in a single pass of the array. You also correctly initialized your variables using the first element of the array, and you set a safe default of 1 for the GCD.

Is it correct? Yes, it passes all test cases. For example, if the array is [2, 5, 6, 9, 10], your code correctly identifies min as 2 and max as 10. The second loop checks i=2, sees both divide evenly, sets GCD to 2, and returns it.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: There are no logical bugs. You correctly handle the edge case where the minimum is 1, as the loop for the GCD simply will not run and will return the default 1.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason: Time complexity is O(n + min) where n is the size of the array and min is the smallest element, because you loop through the array once and then loop up to the minimum value. Space complexity is O(1) because you only use three extra integer variables.

Why my approach works, or why it doesn't: The greatest common divisor of two numbers cannot be larger than the smaller of the two numbers. By checking every integer from 2 up to the minimum number, you are guaranteed to find the largest one that divides both without a remainder.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED

Approach 1: Brute Force GCD (Your Approach)

Idea: Scan the array once to track the smallest and largest numbers. Then, use a loop starting from 1 up to the smallest number to check every single possibility for a common divisor.

C++ code:

C++
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int findGCD(vector<int>& nums) {
        int max_val = nums[0];
        int min_val = nums[0];
        for(int i = 1; i < nums.size(); i++){
            if(nums[i] > max_val) max_val = nums[i];
            if(nums[i] < min_val) min_val = nums[i];
        }
        int gcd_result = 1;
        for(int i = 2; i <= min_val; i++){
            if(max_val % i == 0 && min_val % i == 0){
                gcd_result = i;
            }
        }
        return gcd_result;
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is O(n + min) and space is O(1).

What makes the next approach better: Checking every single number is extremely slow if the numbers are in the millions. We can use an ancient math trick called the Euclidean algorithm to skip the loop entirely.

Approach 2: Manual Euclidean Algorithm

Idea: Find the minimum and maximum elements just like before. To find the GCD, repeatedly replace the larger number with the remainder of the larger divided by the smaller, until the remainder is 0.

C++ code:

C++
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    int calculateGCD(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            int temp = b;
            b = a % b;
            a = temp;
        }
        return a;
    }
public:
    int findGCD(vector<int>& nums) {
        int max_val = nums[0];
        int min_val = nums[0];
        for(int i = 1; i < nums.size(); i++){
            if(nums[i] > max_val) max_val = nums[i];
            if(nums[i] < min_val) min_val = nums[i];
        }
        return calculateGCD(max_val, min_val);
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is O(n + log(min(a, b))) because the modulo operation cuts the numbers down exponentially fast. Space is O(1).

What makes the next approach better: C++ already has built-in functions for finding the minimum, maximum, and GCD, so we do not need to write them from scratch.

Approach 3: Standard Library Functions (Most Optimised)

Idea: Let the C++ standard library do all the work. Use std::minmax_element to find the smallest and largest numbers in one go, and std::gcd to compute the answer.

