WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use? You used Approach 1, finding min and max manually and using a brute-force loop for the GCD.

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem? An interviewer expects Approach 3 to see that you know modern C++ features, but they will almost certainly ask you to write out the Euclidean algorithm from Approach 2 to prove you understand the math.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question: How does the Euclidean algorithm work?

Answer: If a number divides two other numbers, it must also divide their difference. By repeatedly taking the remainder instead of subtracting, we shrink the numbers logarithmically until we hit zero, leaving the greatest common divisor.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence: Never use a brute-force loop to find a greatest common divisor; always use the Euclidean algorithm or std::gcd.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Do not write loops for GCD calculations.

Learn to use std::minmax_element for arrays instead of writing manual tracking variables.

The pattern or idea this question is really about: Array traversal and Number Theory via the Euclidean Algorithm.

THE BEST SOLUTION

C++
#include <vector>
#include <numeric>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int findGCD(vector<int>& nums) {
        auto [min_it, max_it] = minmax_element(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        return gcd(*min_it, *max_it);
    }
};
